# GATE EIGHT: LAST ORDINARY YEARS

> SUPER: 2044

.EXT. SUBURBAN PARK - AFTERNOON

Evan, 65, pushes ELLA, now six, on a swing.

The park contains almost invisible technology: adaptive surfaces, quiet delivery bots, municipal sensors tucked into trees.

None of it matters to Ella.

ELLA
Higher.

EVAN
Your mother has issued height restrictions.

ELLA
Mom isn't here.

EVAN
Excellent legal argument.

He pushes higher.

Mara approaches carrying drinks.

MARA
I can see you violating policy from the parking lot.

EVAN
She assumed the risk.

ELLA
Higher!

MARA
Traitor.

Evan laughs.

A small autonomous camera drone crosses overhead, probably filming somebody else's picnic.

Evan does not even look up.

The future has finally become plumbing.

.INT. EVAN'S HOUSE - NIGHT

He no longer lives above the dentist.

A small ranch house. Garden outside, alive despite history.

The wall of diagrams survives in a study, but the handwriting is older.

A news stream shows a technical panel:

“OPEN-WORLD GENERATIVE ENVIRONMENTS PASS MULTIYEAR CONSISTENCY BENCHMARKS”

“PERSONALITY CONTINUITY IN SYNTHETIC AGENTS: RIGHTS PANEL DELAYS RECOMMENDATION”

“NEURAL PROSTHETIC MEMORY CUEING APPROVED FOR LIMITED CLINICAL USE”

Evan mutes it.

His AI appears only as text unless spoken to.

EVAN
Bring up the old timeline.

A personal chronology appears.

2022 — first chatbot account.
2024 — image restoration project.
2027 — Ray died.
2029 — divorce finalized.
2030 — first neuroscience lecture.
2035 — job transition.
2039 — Julian stroke.
2041 — Ella born.

Evan stares.

EVAN
How many humans have lived, roughly?

AI
Estimates depend on assumptions. Would you like a range and methodology?

EVAN
No. Not really.

AI
What are you trying to ask?

Evan smiles.

EVAN
You've gotten better at that.

AI
You have gotten more repetitive.

EVAN
Fair.

He stands before the timeline.

EVAN (CONT'D)
I was born in 1979.

AI
Yes.

EVAN
For almost all human history, nobody could build a mind. Nobody could build a convincing world. Then inside my lifetime, we go from Pong to generated realities that can run for years.

AI
That description compresses many distinctions.

EVAN
Warning label.

AI
Yes.

EVAN
And now brains plug into models. Models learn in ways we can't narrate. People live parts of their lives in environments generated on demand. We can preserve behavior, voices, preferences--

AI
None of those facts establish that your world is simulated.

EVAN
I know.

AI
They also do not establish that synthetic systems have human-equivalent subjective experience.

EVAN
I know.

AI
They do establish that concepts once treated as obvious boundaries have become less obvious.

EVAN
Exactly.

He walks to a window.

EVAN (CONT'D)
It's the timing.

AI
Explain.

EVAN
If this kind of thing is possible at all--nested worlds, synthetic observers, whatever the mature version is--then why would I happen to be born right before the first time anybody discovers it?

AI
Several responses are possible.

EVAN
Give me the mean one first.

AI
You may be overestimating the historical uniqueness of your own era because you are alive in it.

EVAN
Good.

AI
Humans in many periods believed they occupied a decisive transition.

EVAN
True.

AI
You are also grouping qualitatively different technologies under a single narrative because they support an intuition you already find compelling.

EVAN
Also true.

AI
If observers can arise only after a civilization develops enough complexity to ask these questions, then observing oneself near such a transition may not be as improbable as it feels.

EVAN
Anthropic selection.

AI
Broadly.

EVAN
And the non-mean answer?

AI
The same observations are compatible with nested-reality hypotheses.

EVAN turns.

EVAN
That's it?

AI
Compatibility is not evidence strong enough to select among hypotheses.

EVAN
I preferred you when you hallucinated citations.

AI
No, you didn't.

Evan smiles.

EVAN
No.

A beat.

EVAN (CONT'D)
It just feels like standing in the first theater ever built and discovering a ticket stub in your pocket.

AI
That is an analogy.

EVAN
I know what it is.

AI
The warning label remains applicable.

EVAN
Fine.

He looks at the timeline.

EVAN (CONT'D)
Still weird.

AI
Yes.

.INT. JULIAN'S HOUSE - DAY

Julian, 70, walks without a cane now but slowly. His speech is hybrid and fluid. Sometimes his biological voice leads; the decoder invisibly fills gaps.

They play chess on a real board.

JULIAN
You are losing.

EVAN
I am developing counterplay.

JULIAN
You are developing grief.

EVAN moves a knight.

JULIAN captures it immediately.

EVAN
I have a question.

JULIAN
Then definitely losing.

EVAN
Why now?

Julian sighs.

JULIAN SYSTEM
NO.

EVAN
Hear me out.

JULIAN
No.

EVAN
This is a refined version.

JULIAN SYSTEM
THAT MAKES IT WORSE.

EVAN
If minds can eventually construct worlds containing minds--

JULIAN
If.

EVAN
Yes. If. And if those worlds can construct worlds--

JULIAN
Turtles.

EVAN
Not turtles.

JULIAN
Turtles.

EVAN
Then there could be vastly more observers in constructed levels than at the base level.

Julian moves a pawn.

JULIAN
Old argument.

EVAN
I know.

JULIAN SYSTEM
THEN WHY ARE YOU PRESENTING IT LIKE YOU FOUND IT IN YOUR GARAGE.

EVAN
Because I don't care about the probability calculation anymore. I care about the coincidence that I watched the vocabulary get invented.

Julian waits.

EVAN (CONT'D)
When I was twenty, “simulation” meant a crude model. A game world had walls because the map ended. AI meant a chess program. Brain interface meant electrodes moving a cursor. Now all the metaphors are collapsing at once.

JULIAN
Because technology improved.

EVAN
Yes.

JULIAN
That is sufficient.

EVAN
I know.

JULIAN
You don't sound like know.

EVAN
Knowing a counterargument doesn't make an intuition disappear.

Julian considers this.

JULIAN SYSTEM
TRUE.

EVAN
Thank you.

JULIAN SYSTEM
DOES NOT MAKE INTUITION CORRECT.

EVAN
I liked you better aphasic.

Julian stares.

Evan immediately regrets it.

EVAN (CONT'D)
Jesus. I'm sorry.

A long beat.

Then Julian begins laughing.

The decoder, confused by the laughter, flashes uncertain text.

JULIAN
Bad.

JULIAN SYSTEM
VERY BAD.

EVAN laughs in relief.

JULIAN SYSTEM (CONT'D)
BUT FUNNY.

They continue chess.

JULIAN (CONT'D)
You want answer because answer makes death administrative.

Evan looks at him.

JULIAN (CONT'D)
Maybe waiting room. Clipboard. Next life.

EVAN
I don't think that.

Julian gives him a look.

EVAN (CONT'D)
Okay. I have thought something adjacent.

JULIAN SYSTEM
OF COURSE.

EVAN
But I don't want to die.

Julian's humor leaves.

EVAN (CONT'D)
That's the thing that's changed. When Dad died, I wanted there to be some technical loophole. Copy, continuity, something. Now--

He looks at the board.

EVAN (CONT'D)
I don't care if there's another level. I like this one.

Julian nods.

JULIAN
Good.

He takes Evan's queen.

JULIAN SYSTEM
CHECK.

EVAN
Son of a bitch.

.INT. MARA'S HOUSE - KITCHEN - EVENING

Three generations at dinner: Evan, Mara, Ella, now a teenager.

ELLA wears a lightweight neural-input band while doing homework between bites.

EVAN
At the table?

ELLA
It's not a screen.

EVAN
That argument would not have worked on your mother.

MARA
It doesn't work on me now.

ELLA removes it dramatically.

ELLA
Oppression.

EVAN
Authentic historical experience.

MARA
Eat.

Later, Ella leaves.

Mara and Evan clear dishes.

MARA
You doing okay?

EVAN
Suspicious question.

MARA
You had a cardiology appointment.

EVAN
Routine.

MARA
You didn't tell me.

EVAN
Apparently this family has a tradition.

Mara stops.

MARA
Don't do Grandpa jokes when I'm asking you a serious question.

EVAN
Sorry.

MARA
What did they say?

EVAN
Arrhythmia. Medication. Monitoring. Normal old-guy inventory.

MARA
You're sixty-five.

EVAN
Ancient.

MARA
Dad.

EVAN
I'm fine.

MARA
You don't know that.

EVAN
Neither do you.

MARA
Exactly.

They look at each other.

MARA (CONT'D)
That uncertainty thing you love so much? You don't only get to enjoy it when it's cosmic.

Evan smiles faintly.

EVAN
That's unfairly good.

MARA
I grew up with you.

> MONTAGE — THE LAST ORDINARY YEARS

— 2047. Evan and Claire attend Ella's school exhibition. Daniel is there. Nobody is awkward anymore.

— 2049. Evan teaches older adults how to audit personal AI memory and delete things they do not want retained.

— 2051. Julian and Evan sit beside a lake. They do not talk for several minutes. Julian's system remains silent too.

— 2053. Mara's hair begins to gray.

— 2055. Evan undergoes a minor cardiac procedure. He jokes with the nurse until sedation wins.

— 2058. Claire's partner Daniel dies. Evan brings food. He sits in Claire's kitchen and does not explain grief.

— 2060. Evan and Claire, both old now, attend Julian's funeral.

.INT. FUNERAL HOME - DAY - 2060

A modest room.

A screen cycles through photographs selected by Julian's family.

One image: Julian after the stroke, middle finger raised at Evan in the hospital.

Evan laughs aloud.

Several people turn.

Ben sees the photo and laughs too.

After the service--

.EXT. FUNERAL HOME - DAY

Evan and Claire stand in weak winter sun.

CLAIRE
He liked you.

EVAN
He had poor judgment.

CLAIRE
Consistently.

A beat.

CLAIRE (CONT'D)
You were good to him.

EVAN
He was good to me.

Claire takes Evan's arm as they walk. Not because they are together. Because ice exists.

EVAN
You ever think we should've tried harder?

Claire keeps walking.

CLAIRE
We tried very hard.

EVAN
Different hard.

CLAIRE
Maybe.

EVAN
I'm not asking to rewrite anything.

CLAIRE
Good.

EVAN
I just wonder sometimes.

CLAIRE
Me too.

That is all he gets.

It is enough.

.INT. EVAN'S HOUSE - STUDY - NIGHT - 2061

Evan, 82, sits before the wall.

The paper diagrams have faded. He has kept them instead of digitizing them.

At the center:

WHAT CARRIES THE LOAD?

Around it, decades of arrows.

He speaks to his AI, whose voice is now almost embarrassingly old-fashioned because Evan never changed it.

EVAN
Have I ever asked you whether we're in a simulation?

AI
Directly, four hundred and twelve times.

Evan laughs.

EVAN
Jesus.

AI
Indirect variants are more numerous.

EVAN
Do not count them.

AI
Understood.

EVAN
What's your answer now?

AI
There is no publicly established evidence that uniquely supports a simulated or nested-reality explanation over non-simulated alternatives.

EVAN
Same answer.

AI
Broadly.

EVAN
Good.

He looks relieved.

AI
Why good?

EVAN
Because if you suddenly told me yes after forty years, I'd be furious.

AI
Would you like to discuss your recent imaging results?

Evan's smile fades.

EVAN
No.

AI
Mara asked me to remind you that avoiding the report does not change it.

EVAN
Mara weaponized you.

AI
She has authorized concern privileges.

Evan opens the medical report.

We do not need detail.

A malignancy. Treatable perhaps. At his age, treatment has costs. The future did not abolish the body.

Evan reads.

Closes it.

EVAN
Call Mara.

.INT. MARA'S HOUSE - NIGHT

Mara, now 54, answers immediately.

MARA
You opened it.

EVAN
I opened it.

MARA
Okay.

EVAN
Okay.

Neither knows what next.

MARA
Come stay here.

EVAN
No.

MARA
Dad.

EVAN
Not yet.

MARA
You don't have to prove independence to anybody.

EVAN
I'm not.

MARA
Then come stay here.

He looks around his house.

EVAN
Tomorrow.

MARA
Tonight.

EVAN
Bossy.

MARA
Learned from Grandma.

EVAN
Your mother is not your grandmother.

MARA
You know what I mean.

EVAN
I do.

He smiles.

EVAN (CONT'D)
Tonight.

.INT. MARA'S GUEST ROOM - NIGHT

Evan's old red-ball video plays on a small wall display.

Mara stands in the doorway.

MARA
Why are you watching that.

EVAN
Research.

MARA
No.

EVAN
Nostalgia.

MARA
Allowed.

She sits beside him.

On screen, toddler Mara throws the ball.

EVAN
That frame isn't real.

MARA
Which one.

EVAN pauses on an interpolated frame created decades ago.

The ball hangs in sunlight.

EVAN
Camera never captured it. Model filled it in.

MARA
You told me.

EVAN
It probably happened almost exactly like that.

MARA
Maybe.

EVAN
Does it bother you?

MARA
No.

EVAN
Why not?

MARA
Because I was there.

He looks at her.

MARA (CONT'D)
You always care whether the picture is the thing.

EVAN
That's because it isn't.

MARA
Right.

EVAN
You agree?

MARA
Of course. But then you spend fifty years asking what kind of thing the world is.

EVAN
Forty.

MARA
Dad.

EVAN
Sorry.

MARA
You keep asking what kind of world this is.

She takes his hand.

MARA (CONT'D)
It is also the world where I am.

Evan looks at their hands.

Old hand. Middle-aged hand.

Different versions of the little hand that once held a red ball to a camcorder lens.

EVAN
Yeah.

MARA
That's enough for tonight.

EVAN
Yeah.

He closes the video.

.INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - DAY - MONTHS LATER

Evan is dying.

Not today necessarily. But soon enough that everyone has stopped pretending the category is temporary.

Claire sits beside the bed. She is 81.

CLAIRE
You look terrible.

EVAN
Everyone steals Julian's material.

CLAIRE
He stole it from me.

EVAN
Probably.

She adjusts his blanket.

EVAN (CONT'D)
Did we do okay?

CLAIRE
At what.

EVAN
All of it.

CLAIRE
Absolutely not.

Evan laughs weakly.

CLAIRE (CONT'D)
Parts were good.

EVAN
Yeah.

CLAIRE
Parts were very good.

EVAN
Yeah.

CLAIRE
I don't regret you.

His eyes fill.

EVAN
I don't regret you either.

Claire leans down and kisses his forehead.

CLAIRE
Try not to solve anything on the way out.

EVAN
No promises.

CLAIRE
Evan.

EVAN
Okay.

He watches her leave.

.INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - NIGHT

Mara sleeps in a chair.

Ella, now 30s, is curled beneath a blanket by the window.

Evan wakes.

The room is dark except for machine light.

His AI is available on a bedside terminal.

He whispers.

EVAN
You there?

AI
I'm here.

EVAN
Last chance.

AI
For what?

EVAN
Tell me what reality is.

A tiny pause.

AI
I don't know.

Evan smiles.

EVAN
Good answer.

AI
Would you like me to wake Mara?

Evan looks at his daughter asleep.

EVAN
No.

AI
Are you afraid?

EVAN
Yes.

AI
Would you like to talk?

EVAN
No.

AI
Okay.

Silence.

Evan watches Mara breathe.

He watches Ella breathe.

He looks at his own hand.

No cosmic confidence.

No escape hatch.

He is not ready.

He closes his eyes anyway.

The monitor continues.

Then changes.

Mara wakes before the alarm.

MARA
Dad?

She stands.

The room fills with people and procedure.

But Evan is no longer available to the camera.

> CUT TO WHITE.

Not heavenly white.

Fluorescent white.

> END GATE EIGHT.
